  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Friday)\nIssued at 1121 PM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nThe 00Z upper-air analysis showed an upper-level low located off\nthe Mid-Atlantic Coast, an upper-level trough off the West Coast,\nwith an upper-level ridge present in between these two features\nacross the Lower Mississippi Valley. This ridge resulted in dry\nand pleasant conditions across the Mid-South with late evening\ntemperatures in the 70s. Confidence remains high for dry weather\nthrough early next week and lower confidence in timing of rain\nchances towards the latter half of next week.\n\nThis upper-level ridge will remain in place through early next\nweek, bringing a continuation of dry weather to the area. Highs\nthis weekend will be mostly in the 80s with a few locations in\neast Arkansas and northwest Mississippi approaching 90 degrees.\nTemperatures will fall back into the 50s and 60s at night. Lower\ndewpoint air will mitigate overnight fog development and result\nin comfortable afternoons despite the warm temperatures.\n\nLatest ensemble and deterministic model runs indicate the upper-\nlevel ridge will gradually break down towards the middle of next\nweek as a cutoff upper-level low develops near California and the\nfuture remnants of Hurricane Polo consolidate and move towards\nthe Southern Plains and portions of the Lower Mississippi Valley.\nNBM rain chances have decreased slightly as the ECMWF has been\ntrending a bit slower than the GFS and Canadian solutions. A\nlarge spread in GEFS mid-level heights exists with subsequent\nrain chances needing additional adjustments depending on the\neventual track of this low.",
